CrossOver 26.3 winecoreaudio: render a multichannel stream through AUSpatialMixer when the device has fewer channels.

Against CrossOver 26.3's dlls/winecoreaudio.drv with Highball's patches 0001-0021 applied (the tree engine
r22 is built from). Pairs with mmdevapi-crossover26.3-static-object-order.patch.

What happens without it, measured on an M5 Max with macOS 27.0.1:
- A Core Audio output unit given a 12-channel stream with Wine's bitmap layout (0x2D63F, 7.1.4) accepts it,
  but neither the unit nor an AudioConverter between a 7.1.4 layout and the stereo device mixes anything
  down: a tone in any channel other than front left and right comes out silent. That is why Forza Horizon 6
  lost Anna, dialogue and most of the engine on a Mac's speakers.
- ca_setup_audiounit's "Couldn't set channel layout: -10851" only appears when the number of set bits in
  the channel mask differs from the channel count (12 channels with a stereo mask, or the reverse).
- Apple's own 7.1.4 -> stereo matrix (kAudioFormatProperty_MatrixMixMap) is a plain fold: centre, sides,
  backs and heights at 0.707 to their side, LFE dropped.

With it, a render stream whose format is 32-bit float WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE with three or more channels,
a mask whose bit count matches the channel count and anything beyond FL/FR/FC/LFE, on a device with fewer
output channels, goes through AUSpatialMixer instead of straight into the device unit:
- the mixer takes the stream as an ambience bed (kSpatialMixerSourceMode_AmbienceBed) with a channel
  layout built from the mask (back pair as rear surround when side speakers are present, as surround
  otherwise; top speakers as LeftTopFront and so on) and renders it with
  kSpatializationAlgorithm_UseOutputType;
- the output type follows the device: built-in speakers, headphones on the built-in jack, Bluetooth and
  USB as headphones, anything else as external speakers; HB_SPATIAL=speakers|headphones|external
  overrides the guess and HB_SPATIAL=0 turns the path off;
- on built-in speakers the mixer leaves LFE out, so it is added to the front pair at -6 dB;
- the device unit gets the mixer's stereo output, and the stream's own bookkeeping (padding, latency,
  buffers) is unchanged.

Tested with Forza Horizon 6 on engine r22, M5 Max built-in speakers: the log shows
  winecoreaudio: 12-channel stream (mask 0x2d63f) rendered by AUSpatialMixer for built-in speakers on a 2-channel device
  winecoreaudio: 8-channel stream (mask 0x63f) rendered by AUSpatialMixer for built-in speakers on a 2-channel device
(the spatial audio bed and the game's XAudio2 mastering voice), dialogue and the engine play, and the mix
sounds more detailed than the stereo fold. Built x86_64 from this tree and laid into the environment's
.highball-dlss/wine as x86_64-windows/winecoreaudio.drv and x86_64-unix/winecoreaudio.so, with an
x86_64-unix/ntdll.so link to the engine's (the .so finds ntdll.so through @loader_path).

Still open, and not caused by this patch (it happened before it, and with FAudio 26.10 in xaudio2_9 too):
in FH6 the engine sound sometimes stays silent after changing cars until the camera changes, the car
brakes hard or crashes, or passes about 250 km/h.

